  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 AM EST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\n- Little or no travel impacts through the weekend\n\nA large cut off low will track into the NE CONUS today but the \ncirculation is expansive enough to continue affecting Lower \nMichigan through this afternoon with an area of mid level \nfrontogenesis resulting in mesoscale rainbands overnight with \nsome wet snow mixing in at times under the most dominant bands \nwhere some dynamical cooling occurs.\n\nThe precip winds down this afternoon across western zones and\nthrough the evening further east as the trowal/mid- level f-gen \ntranslates east and is replaced by shortwave ridging before a \nnorthern stream shortwave trough arrives on Saturday. The trough  \nis minoring out as it moves through, therefore little if any \nprecip is expected until a stronger trough arrives on Sunday. \nThere is push of colder air with this trough with 850mb temps \nfalling to minus 5C to minus 8C Sunday night into Monday so some \nsnow could mix in Sunday night before ending, but the chances for \naccumulating snow at this point look to be low. Overall, no real \nimpacts to travel are expected except for some wet roads at times.\n\n- Tempus velox ut ventus\n\nYes,\"time is as fast as the wind\" and after 32 years and 8 months\nin the National Weather Service there is a 100 percent chance \nthat this will be my last shift. It has been an honor and \nprivilege to contribute to the NWS mission of protecting life and \nproperty from destructive weather. While some very talented people\ncontinue that work, I will carry into retirement many fond \nmemories of interacting with people while out on storm surveys, \nspotter and weather safety talks and researching historic tornado \noutbreaks. I look forward to having the opportunity to witness \nour spectacular Lake Michigan sunsets more frequently. Ostuno out!",
